<h2>Before filing: the mediation window</h2>
<p>Section 12A bars the institution of a commercial suit that contemplates no urgent interim relief unless the plaintiff first exhausts pre-institution mediation. The process runs before authorities notified under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, is to be completed within three months (extendable by two with consent), and a settlement reached has the status of an arbitral award on agreed terms. A plaint filed in breach of the mandate is liable to be rejected. The practical choice for a plaintiff is therefore made on day one: either plead and genuinely seek urgent interim relief, or budget for the mediation window.</p>

<h2>The pleading stage: where the hard deadline lives</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Service of summons</strong> — the clock for the written statement starts.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>30 days</strong> — the ordinary period to file the written statement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Up to 120 days</strong> — the court may extend, on costs and recorded reasons, but only up to the 120th day from service.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Day 121</strong> — the right to file stands forfeited; the court cannot take the written statement on record.</div>
</div>
<p>This forfeiture is what most sharply separates commercial suits from ordinary ones, where the timelines for the written statement are directory. Alongside it sit the disclosure obligations: all documents in the party's power and possession must be filed with the plaint or written statement with a statement of truth, and documents not disclosed cannot ordinarily be relied upon without leave.</p>

<h2>From pleadings to trial: case management</h2>
<p>Order XV-A, inserted for commercial disputes, converts the court into a scheduler. The first case management hearing is to be held within four weeks of the filing of the affidavit of admission and denial of documents. At that hearing the court frames issues, fixes dates for evidence and arguments, and passes orders it considers necessary to decide the suit expeditiously.</p>
