  <tr><td>Inspection of documents</td><td>Within 30 days of completion of pleadings</td><td>Extendable by the court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Admission/denial of documents</td><td>Within 15 days of inspection</td><td>Extendable by the court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>First case management hearing (O. XV-A)</td><td>Within 4 weeks of admission/denial affidavit</td><td>Directory but policed by costs</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Closure of oral arguments</td><td>Not beyond 6 months from the first case management hearing</td><td>Court must endeavour; adjournments restricted</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Written arguments</td><td>Before oral arguments; revised version within one week after</td><td>Mandatory filing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Judgment (O. XX)</td><td>Within 90 days of conclusion of arguments</td><td>Addressed to the court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Appeal (s. 13)</td><td>60 days from judgment/order</td><td>Limitation period</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Disposal of appeal (s. 14)</td><td>Endeavour within 6 months</td><td>Directory</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Evidence and arguments on a schedule</h2>
<p>Within the case management framework, examination-in-chief goes on affidavit, cross-examination is to proceed day to day wherever possible, and the court may fix time limits for oral submissions. Adjournments on the ground that counsel is engaged elsewhere are expressly discouraged, and costs — actual and realistic under the amended Section 35 regime — are the sanction of first resort for a party that slips the schedule.</p>

<h2>Which deadlines bite, and on whom</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Fatal for the party</h4><p>The 120-day written statement cap, the 60-day appeal limitation (subject to the law of condonation), and the bar on relying on undisclosed documents without leave. These are the traps that lose cases.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Binding but court-facing</h4><p>The 90-day judgment rule and the six-month appellate disposal target direct the court rather than the litigant. A party cannot be penalised for the court's delay, but can legitimately press for listing by reference to them.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Managed by costs</h4><p>Inspection, admission/denial and case-management dates are extendable — but extensions travel with costs, and a record of delay colours the court's discretion at every later stage.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practical takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>A defendant served in a commercial suit should treat the service date as the most important date in the case and diarise day 30 and day 120 at once.</li>
    <li>A plaintiff should assemble complete documentary disclosure before filing — the statement of truth and disclosure rules punish afterthoughts.</li>
    <li>Both sides should attend case management hearings with a realistic calendar, because the schedule fixed there becomes the law of the case.</li>
    <li>Where judgment is reserved beyond ninety days, a mention seeking pronouncement, courteously made, is legitimate practice.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note">
  <p>This explainer describes the statutory scheme applicable to commercial disputes of a specified value under the Commercial Courts Act, 2015. It is general information, not legal advice on any particular suit.</p>
